##2.5 Discussion: why does TWFE fail in staggered settings?

#a)
#In a TWFE both unit and time fixed effect are included to estimate the treatment
#effect. This can produce misleading results in staggered DiD settings, because 
#treatment effect can be heterogeneous over time and units.
#The “forbidden comparison” problem occurs in staggered DiD settings, when the 
#TWFE model uses already-treated units for later-treated units as a control.
#As a control group; never-treated units, not-yet-treated units, and already-treated
#units are used. This can lead to problems if the treatment effect is heterogeneous 
#over time and cohorts, because the control is itself affected by the treatment.



#b)
#The estimator for the TWFE is -0.0365 vs. Callaway-Santanna estimate is  -0.0418.
#They differ only by a small margin and point into the same direction. 
#As discussed in the last question TWFE can lead to misleading results in our case.
#Therefore, Callaway-Santanna estimate appears to be the more credible option.








